+# Running KV-aware routing + kvd on SGLang
+
+```{admonition} One-pager
+:class: tip
+**What:** how to run a PD-disaggregated SGLang deployment with **KV-aware
+routing** and **tiered KV offload (kvd)** both on, in production. **Why a
+separate page:** the feature pages tell you the flags; this one tells you which
+of them exist only to make an *experiment* cheap, what to set instead in
+production, how to prove each switch actually took effect, and which numbers are
+safe to tune.
+```
+
+Everything below was verified on GLM-5.2-MXFP4 across two MI355X nodes, PD over
+Mooncake RDMA, DP-attention on both legs, both features on.
+
+## 1. Experiment-only settings — do not ship these
+
+These appear in the reproduction scripts. Each exists to make a test cheap or to
+route around a lab constraint, and each is **wrong for production**.
+
+| Setting | Why it was used | Production instead |
+|---|---|---|
+| `MC_FORCE_TCP=1` | Both PD legs on **one host**, where Mooncake RDMA must loop back across rails (`ionic_0 → ionic_4`) and fails with `transport retry counter exceeded`. TCP is the known-good, slower path. | **Drop it.** Put the legs on different hosts and use RDMA. This flag costs you most of the PD transfer bandwidth. |
+| `--hicache-size 8` (GB) | A small RAM tier so the working set spills to L3 and the test actually exercises disk. | Size to the real hot set — typically **32–128 GB**. See §4. |
+| `--max-bytes 8G` on the daemon | Same reason: force spillover. | Match the host's spare RAM, typically **64–256 GB**. |
+| `--long-path /tmp/kvd-long` | Somewhere writable in a scratch container. | A real NVMe mount. `/tmp` is usually tmpfs — that is **RAM pretending to be disk**, so your L3 tier silently competes with L2 for the same memory. |
+| Qwen3-1.7B as the model | Cuts a ~30 min GLM-5.2 cold start to ~2 min, so the wiring loop could run 5 rounds in an afternoon. The thing under test was the wiring, not the model. | Your real model. A small model is excellent for **wiring** bugs and tells you nothing about capacity. |
+| `docker cp` of the host `libionic.so` | The scripts predate the image entrypoint. | Nothing — bind-mount the host library at `/host-libionic/libionic.so` and `infera-inject-host-ionic` does it. See `deploy/docker/scripts/infera_inject_host_ionic.sh`. |
+| Two TP4 workers packed onto one node | Gives the KV-aware scorer ≥2 workers per role to choose between, on one node's worth of GPUs. | One worker per node at full TP, and as many nodes as you have. |
+| `--kv-events-bind` / `--kv-snapshot-port` set per leg | Two workers on **one host** collide on the shared defaults (`5557`, `8801`). | Only needed when co-locating workers. One worker per host uses the defaults. |
+
+```{admonition} The port-collision trap that looks like a healthy leg
+:class: warning
+`--kv-snapshot-port` defaults to `8801` on **every** worker. Two workers on one
+host: the second logs `ready to roll`, *then* dies during etcd registration with
+`[Errno 98] address already in use`. The engine looks up and the worker never
+appears in the router. If a leg is "up" but never registers, check for `Errno
+98` **after** the ready line.
+```
+
+## 2. Recommended production configuration — all switches on
+
+Three roles, one image (`deploy/docker/Dockerfile.sglang.kvaware-kvd`).
+
+**Per host: the kvd daemon.** One per host; every engine on that host shares it.
+
+```bash
+python -m infera.kvd \
+  --socket /run/infera-kvd/kvd.sock \
+  --max-bytes 128G \
+  --long-path /nvme/kvd-long --long-bytes 2T \
+  --io-mode auto \
+  --log-level INFO
+```
+
+**Each engine leg** (prefill shown; decode is the same minus
+`--disaggregation-bootstrap-port`). Launch through the **infera wrapper** —
+`sglang.launch_server` bypasses all of the kvaware/kvd wiring:
+
+```bash
+python -m infera.engine.sglang \
+  --model-path <model> --host <routable-ip> --port 30000 \
+  --advertise-host <routable-ip> \
+  --etcd-endpoint <etcd>:2379 --discovery-backend etcd \
+  --request-transport http --kv-event-transport zmq \
+  --disaggregation-mode prefill \
+  --disaggregation-transfer-backend mooncake \
+  --disaggregation-ib-device <active-rails> \
+  --disaggregation-bootstrap-port 8998 \
+  --dp-size 8 --enable-dp-attention --ep-size 8 \
+  --infera-kvd-socket /run/infera-kvd/kvd.sock \
+  --hicache-size 64
+```
+
+`PYTHONHASHSEED=0` is baked into the image; if you build your own, set it.
+
+**The router**, with the PD-aware weights:
+
+```bash
+python -m infera.server --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8100 \
+  --discovery-backend etcd --etcd-endpoint <etcd>:2379 \
+  --request-transport http --kv-event-transport zmq \
+  --router-policy kv-aware --router-tokenizer-path <model> \
+  --kv-prefill-overlap-weight 20.0 \
+  --kv-decode-overlap-weight 2.0 \
+  --kvd-socket-path /run/infera-kvd/kvd.sock
+```
+
+```{admonition} Three defaults that are wrong for a PD deployment
+:class: important
+1. **`--kv-overlap-weight` defaults to `1.0`**, and the prefill/decode weights
+   fall back to it. At 1.0 the cache term barely outweighs load, so you get
+   nearly round-robin behaviour and will conclude kv-aware "does nothing". Set
+   the per-role weights explicitly (§4).
+2. **`--no-enable-kv-events` on a decode leg silently disables kvd there**,
+   because infera only re-enables the decode radix cache when KV events are on.
+   Leave KV events on.
+3. **`--hicache-ratio` (default 2.0) sizes the host pool off
+   `max_total_num_tokens`** — it asked for 355 GB *per DP rank* on a small model.
+   Use `--hicache-size <GB>`.
+```
+
+DP-attention must be **symmetric** across the pair (same `--dp-size`/`--ep-size`
+on both legs) or the KV shard layout does not match across the transfer.
+
+## 3. Verifying each switch actually took effect
+
+Every check below has a **negative** form — something that also looks like
+success but is not. That is the whole point: all three of these features fail
+*silently*, degrading to a slower-but-correct path.
+
+### kvd is wired
+
+```bash
+grep -E "infera-kvd adapter connected|hierarchical=True" <engine-log>
+```
+
+Want: `infera-kvd adapter connected to /run/infera-kvd/kvd.sock` and a
+`Tree cache initialized: ... HiRadixCache ... hierarchical=True`.
+
+Cheap pre-check: `--infera-kvd-socket` **fails fast** (5 s probe). If the daemon
+is unreachable the engine refuses to start, so a running engine has *a* daemon —
+but not necessarily one that is being used.
+
+### kvd is actually serving
+
+```bash
+python -m infera.kvd.statctl --socket /run/infera-kvd/kvd.sock
+```
+
+Drive a repeated long shared prefix, then want `sets_total > 0` followed by
+`gets_total > 0` with `hits_total` climbing.
+
+```{admonition} A speed-up does NOT prove kvd did anything
+:class: warning
+SGLang's in-GPU radix cache serves a repeated prefix without ever touching L3.
+In our run the reuse phase was **2.7× faster** while kvd's counters stayed at
+**zero** — the win was entirely the GPU cache.
+
+To attribute reuse to kvd specifically: **restart the engine** (which empties
+the GPU cache) and replay the same prefixes. Hits should climb with **no new
+sets**. Ours went `+170 hits / 0 new sets` — that reuse could only have come
+from L3.
+```
+
+`misses_total > 0` means the scheduler and worker derived different chunk keys —
+almost always a `PYTHONHASHSEED` mismatch.
+
+### KV events are flowing (the kv-aware input)
+
+```bash
+grep "KV plane up" <engine-log>
+```
+
+Also confirm each leg picked a **distinct** publisher port. Two legs sharing one
+means one of them is dead:
+
+```bash
+grep -o "kv-events.*:[0-9]*" <prefill-log> <decode-log>
+```
+
+### KV-aware routing is actually steering
+
+Load a prefix on one worker, then send the same prefix again and see where it
+lands. The discriminating test is a **policy flip** on identical traffic:
+
+| policy | requests to worker A | to worker B |
+|---|---|---|
+| `kv-aware` | 17 | 0 |
+| `round-robin` | +6 | +8 |
+
+Round-robin splits; kv-aware pins every repeat onto the worker holding the
+prefix. If your kv-aware run also splits evenly, the most likely cause is
+`--kv-overlap-weight` left at its `1.0` default.
+
+The router logs its arithmetic per decision:
+
+```
+cache_hits=… request_blocks=… active_blocks=… w_overlap=…
+```
+
+`w_overlap=1.00` when you expected `20.00` means the flag did not reach the
+policy.
+
+### The decode leg really has kvd (PD only)
+
+```bash
+grep "disaggregation-decode-enable-radix-cache" <decode-log>
+```
+
+Absent → the decode leg has no hierarchical cache, whatever else you passed.
+
+## 4. Tunable numbers, and what to set them to
+
+### Router: overlap weights
+
+`cost(w) = overlap_weight × (request_blocks − hits(w)) + active_blocks(w)`
+
+Cache locality vs. load balance, on one axis. Higher = chase the warm worker
+harder; lower = spread the load.
+
+| Flag | Default | Recommended | Why |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `--kv-prefill-overlap-weight` | falls back to `1.0` | **20.0** | Prefill is compute-bound; a hit skips an entire prefill pass, which dominates everything else. Bias hard. |
+| `--kv-decode-overlap-weight` | falls back to `1.0` | **2.0** | Decode is memory-bound on KV. A prefill-time hit does little for the decode loop, and even latency matters more. Bias weakly. |
+| `--kv-overlap-weight` | `1.0` | 1.0–2.0 | Mixed (non-PD) pools and the fallback for the two above. |
+
+Tune from the recommended values, and move **one at a time**:
+
+- Prefill workers hot-spotting while others idle → lower the prefill weight
+  (20 → 10). The load term is in *blocks*, so with long prompts it can take a
+  large weight before locality wins at all.
+- Prefill hit rate flat and traffic spread evenly → raise it (20 → 40).
+- Decode tail latency spiking → lower the decode weight toward 1.0.
+
+Client `cache_control` also scales the weight per request: **long retention
+×2.0**, **explicit `none` ×0.1**. So a `retention=long` request effectively sees
+prefill weight 40 at the default. Account for that before concluding your
+weight is too high.
+
+```{admonition} Our measurement of these weights is incomplete
+:class: note
+The 17/0-vs-round-robin result was measured with **one prefill worker** and a
+**single** shared prefix. That demonstrates the mechanism works; it does not
+validate `20.0` as the optimum, and it never tested affinity between
+*competing* prefixes. Treat 20.0/2.0 as a documented starting point and measure
+on your own traffic.
+```
+
+### kvd daemon: tier sizes
+
+| Flag | Default | Recommended | Notes |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `--max-bytes` (L2, host RAM) | `8G` | **64–256G** | The hot tier. Too small and everything falls through to L3 on every miss. Leave headroom: this is pinned host RAM competing with the engine's own host allocations. |
+| `--long-bytes` (L3) | — | **1–4T** | Cheap. Size to the working set you want to survive restarts. |
+| `--long-path` | — | a real NVMe mount | Not `/tmp` — that is usually tmpfs, i.e. RAM. |
+| `--long-paths` | — | one path per NVMe | Striped L3. Reads fan out across shards; N inodes on N devices give real parallelism where a single device does not. `--long-bytes` is then the **total** budget. |
+| `--io-mode` | `auto` | `auto` | `auto` probes for O_DIRECT eligibility. Force `direct`/`buffered` only to diagnose. |
+
+```{admonition} Bind-mounted L3 and O_DIRECT
+:class: note
+A bind-mounted L3 directory used to classify as buffered even on qualifying
+NVMe, because `findmnt` reports the source as `/dev/md0[/mnt/...]` and the
+bracketed form is not a block device. Fixed — but if you are on an older build,
+this is why a container-hosted kvd never used O_DIRECT.
+```
+
+### Engine: host cache pool
+
+| Flag | Default | Recommended | Notes |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `--hicache-size` (GB, per rank) | — | **32–128** | Absolute; overrides the ratio. **Prefer this.** Note it is **per DP rank**: with `--dp-size 8`, `--hicache-size 64` allocates 512 GB on the host. |
+| `--hicache-ratio` | `2.0` | avoid | Scales off `max_total_num_tokens`. Asked for 355 GB/rank on a 1.7 B model. **Below 1.5 it silently disables L3 prefetch** — `prefetch_capacity_limit = 0.8 × (host_pool − device_pool)` goes to zero. |
+
+### What we did not measure
+
+Stated plainly so nobody reads a recommendation as a benchmark:
+
+- **kvd latency benefit** — not observed. The reload phase (0.76 s) was no
+  faster than cold (0.71 s). kvd demonstrably *served* the blocks; it did not
+  demonstrably make anything faster at this scale.
+- **Concurrency vs. locality** — every request in the routing test was
+  sequential, so the `active_blocks` load term never actually pushed back
+  against the cache term. The interesting behaviour of these weights is under
+  load, and we did not observe it.
+- **Competing prefixes** — one shared prefix only.
+- **True NVMe O_DIRECT L3** — the lab's NVMe drives were unmounted; L3 ran on a
+  filesystem that classified as buffered.
+
+## Related
+
+- [KV Cache Offload](../features/kv_cache_offload.md) — the kvd feature page,
+  including the SGLang section.
+- [KV-Aware Routing](../features/kv_aware_routing.md) — the routing feature page.
